# engine-gpu — the vLLM image of the two GPU lanes.
#
# ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
# ║  DECLARED AS NOT VERIFIED (2026-08-29).                          ║
# ║                                                                  ║
# ║  This file has NOT been built and the image it describes has NOT ║
# ║  been run. It was written on a machine with no GPU and no        ║
# ║  cluster, from measurements taken against the upstream build     ║
# ║  definitions (each one cited below, with its URL), and NOT from  ║
# ║  an execution. Every version pin here is defensible; none of     ║
# ║  them is confirmed by a running engine.                          ║
# ║                                                                  ║
# ║  An image that claims to be tested and is not is worse than one  ║
# ║  that warns. The first build is the verification, and the        ║
# ║  protocol (docs/protocols/ai-subsystem.md §3) says what to watch ║
# ║  while it runs and what to write down afterwards.                ║
# ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
#
# ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL ────────────────────────────────────
#
# The image it replaces was assembled BY HAND, in a ceremony that was
# explicitly dismantled and recorded as not reproducible from the
# internet: a frozen virtualenv packaged as OCI layers. It passed the
# same rite as any third party — blocking scan before the push,
# signature by digest, the key never leaving the cluster — but if the
# internal registry were lost and that virtualenv were lost, both GPU
# engines would be unrecoverable without redoing the work from scratch.
# That is a single point of failure with no backup and no build, and it
# was the last thing in the platform that could not be rebuilt from
# source. This file is what closes it: the image is BORN from a
# Containerfile, from a base that went through the mirror, and it goes
# through the same scan and the same signature as everything else.
#
# ── WHY EVERY VERSION IS PINNED, AND WHERE EACH NUMBER COMES FROM ──
#
# The target is a Blackwell CONSUMER card: compute capability sm_120
# (`12.0` in the arch lists below). That is not the same architecture as
# the datacenter Blackwell (sm_100), and a build that says "Blackwell"
# is not necessarily a build that carries sm_120 kernels. Each pin below
# was measured against the upstream build definition rather than taken
# from a release note's summary.
#
#   torch 2.11.0 FROM THE cu130 INDEX. (It said cu128 until 2026-09-01;
#   see «CU130, AND THE ENGINE ITSELF SAID SO» further down for the
#   measurement that moved it. The reasoning below is unchanged and is
#   why the INDEX is named at all — only the number moved.)
#   PyTorch's own wheel build script
#   decides the compiled architectures by CUDA version:
#
#       TORCH_CUDA_ARCH_LIST="7.5;8.0;8.6;9.0;10.0"
#       12.6) ... (no 12.0)
#       12.8) TORCH_CUDA_ARCH_LIST="${TORCH_CUDA_ARCH_LIST};12.0"  # +Blackwell
#
#       measured 2026-08-29 at
#       raw.githubusercontent.com/pytorch/pytorch/v2.11.0/.ci/manywheel/build_cuda.sh
#
#   So the CUDA VARIANT is the load-bearing part, not the torch version:
#   the cu126 wheel of this very same 2.11.0 has NO sm_120 kernels and
#   fails at the first matmul with "no kernel image is available". That
#   is why the index is named explicitly below and why the local version
#   (`+cu130`) is written into the pin: `pip install torch==2.11.0` off
#   plain PyPI resolves to whatever CUDA variant is default that month.
#
#   vllm 0.25.1, which REQUIRES torch==2.11.0 (measured 2026-08-29 on
#   pypi.org/pypi/vllm/0.25.1/json) — so the two pins are one decision,
#   not two. vLLM's own release build compiles for the same list:
#
#       ARG torch_cuda_arch_list='7.5 8.0 8.6 8.9 9.0 10.0 11.0 12.0'
#       measured at github.com/vllm-project/vllm/v0.25.1/docker/Dockerfile
#
#   and its CMakeLists accepts 12.0 as a supported arch. This line and
#   not a newer one because the ENGINE MANIFESTS ARE WRITTEN AGAINST ITS
#   CLI: `--attention-config` (the flag that replaced the attention
#   backend environment variable) and `--override-generation-config`.
#   Moving to a newer vLLM is moving those flags too, and that is a
#   change with a measurement behind it, not a version bump.
#
#   python 3.12, because that is the base the platform already mirrors
#   and because vllm 0.25.1 declares `>=3.10,<3.15`.
#
# ── WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT INSTALLED ─────────────────────────────
#
# NVCC. This image has a C compiler (for Triton's JIT kernels) and no
# CUDA compiler, and that is a size decision with a consequence that has
# to be known rather than discovered: any path that compiles CUDA at
# runtime dies here. That is exactly what happened to the FP8 route on
# this card — bmm_fp8 -> fp8_gemm_sm100 -> FlashInfer's sm120 CUTLASS,
# compiled JIT — with
#
#     RuntimeError: Could not find nvcc and default cuda_home=
#     '/usr/local/cuda' doesn't exist
#
# The engines therefore serve bf16 activations and an int4 or bf16
# weight format, never FP8. Upstream agrees about where the edge is:
# vLLM's CMakeLists enables its FP8 Marlin kernels for `8.9;12.0f`, and
# the `12.0f` family target requires CUDA >= 13.0 — on a CUDA 12.8 build
# it falls back to `12.0a;12.1a`, which is another way of saying the FP8
# path on this card is the one that is least travelled. Adopting FP8
# means putting nvcc in this image, which is a decision to take with a
# measurement, not a line to add quietly.
#
# ── THE BASE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `__FROM_PYTHON__` is resolved against the INTERNAL registry, by
# digest, before this file is built: the base has to be one the mirror
# already pulled, scanned and re-signed, because everything downstream
# of it runs in a namespace where Kyverno demands our signature. The
# digest cannot be written here by hand — the mirror rebuilds the
# manifest as it copies, so the public digest in images.txt is not the
# one that pulls. `aegis ai images` resolves it (it asks the live
# registry, the same read `aegis image from` does) and phase 87 refuses
# to deploy while any AI image is still unresolved.
FROM __FROM_PYTHON__

# libc6-dev IS PART OF THE COMPILER, and leaving it out is what
# `--no-install-recommends` does. Measured 2026-09-02: with gcc-14
# installed and CC pointing at it, Triton's first JIT died with
#
#     cuda.h:56:10: fatal error: stdlib.h: No such file or directory
#
# A compiler with no standard headers is not a compiler, and the
# failure said so four frames deep inside a CalledProcessError, after
# the model had already been loaded — sixty seconds of work to reach a
# missing header. The flag is right and stays: what it dropped is
# named explicitly instead.
#
# gcc-14 AND NOT `gcc`. Triton compiles its kernels JIT at the engine's
# first start and dies with no compiler; the engine manifests set
# CC=/usr/bin/gcc-14 explicitly because the Debian package installs the
# versioned binary and the bare `gcc` symlink belongs to a different
# package. Installing it here and naming it there is one decision in two
# files, and the manifests say so at the CC line.
#
# --no-install-recommends, and the lists deleted in the SAME layer: a
# separate `RUN rm` leaves the 40 MB of apt lists in the layer below,
# where they still ship and still get scanned.
# `apt-get upgrade` FIRST, and it is not hygiene theatre: the blocking
# scan measured 2026-09-01 refused this image over libssl3t64
# CVE-2026-14456 (HIGH, fixed in 3.5.7-1~deb13u2) which the base still
# ships at 3.5.6. It is the same reason the platform's own bases exist
# and run `apk upgrade` on top of what the mirror gave them: a base is
# a floor, not a promise, and the fix is upstream and available — so it
# is applied, not excepted.
RUN apt-get update \
 && apt-get upgrade -y --no-install-recommends \
 && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends gcc-14 libgomp1 libc6-dev \
 && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# THE VIRTUALENV IS AT A FIXED PATH because the manifests name it:
# `command: ["/opt/ai/venv/bin/python", ...]`. A venv and not a
# --system install so that the engine's interpreter is unambiguous and
# so that a future stage could copy the tree wholesale.
ENV VENV=/opt/ai/venv
# The venv is created and its OWN tooling is brought up to date in the
# same layer. `python -m venv` seeds pip, setuptools and wheel from
# what the base image froze, and on 2026-09-01 those three carried
# three of the four findings that blocked this build: setuptools
# CVE-2025-47273, wheel CVE-2026-24049 and jaraco.context
# CVE-2026-23949 (vendored inside setuptools). All three are fixed
# upstream; none of them needs an exception.
RUN python -m venv "$VENV" \
 && "$VENV/bin/pip" install --no-cache-dir --upgrade pip setuptools wheel
ENV PATH="/opt/ai/venv/bin:${PATH}"

# CU130, AND THE ENGINE ITSELF SAID SO. This was cu128 until
# 2026-09-01, reasoned and never verified — the sign on this file said
# as much. The first image that ever ran crashed on import with
# `ImportError: libcudart.so.13`, and the answer was in vllm 0.25.1's
# own metadata: it requires `nvidia-cutlass-dsl[cu13]`. The wheel is
# compiled against CUDA 13, so its extension looks for CUDA 13's
# runtime, and a torch built against 12.8 ships libcudart.so.12. Two
# correct choices that do not speak to each other.
#
# Blackwell (sm_120) is served by CUDA 13 as it is by 12.8, so the
# reason cu128 was chosen is not lost by moving: what is gained is that
# the runtime under vLLM is the one vLLM was built on.
#
# TORCH FIRST AND ON ITS OWN, from the cu130 index. Two reasons and both
# are about determinism: pip would otherwise resolve torch from plain
# PyPI while satisfying vllm's `torch==2.11.0`, and PyPI's default CUDA
# variant is whatever upstream shipped as default that month — the one
# thing that decides whether this image has sm_120 kernels. Installing
# it first, by exact local version, from the named index, makes the
# question have one answer.
#
# torchvision comes along because vllm asks for it and the cu130 index
# carries it (measured 2026-09-01: the cp312 x86_64 wheel of
# 0.26.0+cu130 is published); torchaudio is resolved by the vllm
# install below, from PyPI, because the pytorch index does not publish
# a 2.11.0 of it and it carries nothing that depends on the card.
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir \
      --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu130 \
      torch==2.11.0+cu130 \
      torchvision==0.26.0+cu130

# THE TWO INSTALLS STAY IN TWO LAYERS, and this is a decision, not an
# oversight — it is written here because the temptation to merge them
# is real and arrives with a stopwatch in hand.
#
# Measured 2026-09-01 on build #16: of sixteen minutes of build, nine
# were kaniko photographing the filesystem. It does not use kernel
# layers like Docker: after every RUN it walks the whole tree and
# hashes each file to work out the diff, and with torch and CUDA
# unpacked that is tens of thousands of files, twice. Merging these
# two RUNs removes one of those two passes.
#
# It was not merged, and the reason is the one this whole file is
# written for. What that layer boundary buys is that a finding — a
# CVE, a wrong version, a file that should not be there — can be
# attributed to the step that introduced it: the image manifest says
# which layer brought torch and which brought vLLM, and a scanner
# reports the DiffID of every finding. That is worth more than minutes,
# because the expensive thing in this subsystem has never been a
# failure: it is a failure that surfaces far from its cause.
#
# The saving was also never measured. The remaining snapshot would run
# over a larger tree, so it is less than it looks. A permanent property
# that helps the day something breaks is not traded for an unmeasured
# saving that happens once per build.
#
# This does NOT contradict the apt rule above. What a step creates and
# discards is discarded in the SAME layer, or it ships in the one
# below. What two different decisions do belongs in two different
# steps. The two rules are about different things.
#
# vLLM, with torch ALREADY satisfied so the resolver cannot replace it.
# AND PIP ITSELF GOES. Measured 2026-09-01, after three wrong
# guesses: the last two findings the scan refused —msgpack 1.1.x and
# setuptools 70.3.0— were never the INSTALLED packages. They are the
# copies pip carries inside `pip/_vendor/`, declared in its own
# vendor.txt, and they have no dist-info, which is why a `find` for
# dist-info directories came back empty and why upgrading setuptools
# could never touch them.
#
# So the fix is not a newer version, it is not shipping an installer
# in a runtime image at all: nothing here installs packages once it
# runs, and the engine's own dependencies stay exactly as resolved.
# Both interpreters, because the scan reads both.
#
# THE VERSION IS BOUNDED, and that is not fussiness: a bare
# `--upgrade setuptools` installed 84.0.0 on 2026-09-01 and BROKE the
# constraints the engine itself declares — torch asks for <82 and vllm
# for <81. pip reported that as a warning and carried on, which is the
# worst outcome: a green build carrying an engine whose dependencies
# do not agree. 78.1.1 is the fixed version AND satisfies both.
#
# Two interpreters get touched, because the scan sees both: the venv
# (which is what runs) and the base's system python, whose setuptools
# 70.3.0 carries the same finding and answers to no constraint here.
#
# The setuptools upgrade is LAST, and the order is the whole point.
# Measured 2026-09-01: upgrading it beside the venv creation was
# undone — installing torch pulls setuptools 78.1.0 back in as a build
# dependency, and with it the vendored jaraco.context 5.3.0 and wheel
# 0.45.1 that the scan refuses. Three findings that are really one, and
# the fix is not a different command but a later one.
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir vllm==0.25.1 \
 && pip install --no-cache-dir --upgrade "setuptools>=78.1.1,<81" msgpack \
 && /usr/local/bin/pip install --no-cache-dir --upgrade setuptools \
 && python -m pip uninstall -y pip \
 && /usr/local/bin/python -m pip uninstall -y pip \
 && python -c "import vllm, torch; print('vllm', vllm.__version__, 'torch', torch.__version__)"

# A NAME FOR uid 1000, and it is not cosmetics.
#
# The manifests already set HOME explicitly, with a comment saying this
# user has no passwd entry — which covered every library that asks
# «where is my home». torch 2.11 asks a different question: its
# inductor builds a cache path from getpass.getuser(), and getpwuid()
# raises for a uid nobody declared. Measured 2026-09-01: the engine
# died on import with `KeyError: getpwuid(): uid not found: 1000`,
# with HOME set correctly the whole time.
#
# So the hole was patched for a CASE and not for the CLASS. Declaring
# the user answers the question once, for every library that ever asks
# it, instead of one environment variable per caller. The home here
# matches the HOME the manifests set, so the two agree instead of
# merely not colliding.
RUN printf 'aegis:x:1000:1000::%s:/usr/sbin/nologin\n' '/cache' >> /etc/passwd \
 && printf 'aegis:x:1000:\n' >> /etc/group

# uid numeric, like the rest of the house: the Deployment declares
# runAsNonRoot + runAsUser 1000 and this is the belt as well as the
# braces. The user has no passwd entry, which is why the manifests set
# HOME explicitly — otherwise Triton's cache lands somewhere the
# read-only root filesystem refuses.
USER 1000:1000

EXPOSE 8000

# NO ENTRYPOINT AND NO CMD, deliberately. The two engines run the same
# image with different arguments, and the manifests carry the whole
# command line — the model, the memory fraction, the attention backend.
# An entrypoint here would be a second place where those decisions live,
# and the manifests are the place where they are already explained.
